  "devdoc": {
    "kind": "dev",
    "methods": {
      "constructor": {
        "params": {
          "_implementation": "The V4 implementation new proxies are created over.         Must have code and answer `factory()`.",
          "_legacyImplementations": "The V2 and V3 implementations existing        proxies were built over. Each must have code. They are recorded as        recognisable, not approved: they carry no upgrade gate, which is        exactly why nothing is created over them here.",
          "_protocolSafe": "The only address that may set the implementation or        register a module. Must have code."
        }
      },
      "createModule(address,address[],address[])": {
        "details": "Callable by `safe` itself, or by the protocol safe on its behalf.      Idempotent per safe: a second call returns the proxy already      recorded, deploys nothing and emits nothing. That includes a module      recorded by `registerModule`: once a safe has an entry in the index,      this function returns it and never deploys over it."
      },
      "moduleAddress(address)": {
        "details": "The index is `safeModules[safe]`, so this answers the entry no matter      how it got there: `createModule` pushes the proxy it deployed, and      `registerModule` pushes a module deployed elsewhere. Once there is an      entry, `createModule` returns it, deploys nothing and emits nothing,      so a registered module is the module for good and this view cannot      drift from it. With no entry it is the prediction under      `latestImplementation`, which names a create that has not happened."
      },
      "moduleAddressForVersion(address,uint256)": {
        "details": "1-based over `implementationHistory`; reverts `InvalidVersion`      outside `1..implementationCount()`. This answers for a generation,      not for the module the safe has: only `moduleAddress` follows the      module that exists."
      },
      "registerModule(address,address,address)": {
        "details": "The protocol safe's write, never the publication's: this is the      protocol saying which module is a publication's module, and a      publication's Safe cannot say it about itself.      Zero addresses and a module without code are refused. An existing      entry is final: re-registering the same module returns quietly, and a      different module reverts `AlreadyRegistered(safe, existing)`. Nothing      overwrites the index, so a reader that resolved a module once cannot      have it swapped under it.      A module is accepted on either of two facts:        a) it answers `factory()` with this address, and `implementation`           is one this factory approved — a proxy deployed here, whose           upgrades this contract governs; or        b) it does not, and `implementation` is one the constructor           recorded as legacy — a V2 or V3 publication at a plain-CREATE           address it cannot leave, whose proxy was upgraded to V4 in place.      `implementation` is named by the caller because a contract cannot read      another contract's storage: the ERC-1967 slot      (`0x360894a1…382bbc`) is legible over RPC, not from inside this      contract. So the legacy branch checks that the protocol safe named an      implementation this factory recorded, for a module that does not claim      this factory. It is a recognition step, not a proof of what the      module's code does: a proxy upgraded since naming that implementation      runs something else behind the same slot, and nothing here inspects      it. A module that claims this factory is the only case where the      approval set above actually governs the code.      `implementation` in `ModuleRegistered` is the argument, recorded so the      index says which implementation the protocol was told to expect."
      },
      "setImplementation(address)": {
        "details": "Only the protocol safe. Existing proxies are unaffected, and so is      their address: `moduleAddress` moves, `moduleAddressForVersion` does      not. The new implementation also becomes a legal upgrade target for      every V4 module already deployed, without touching any of them."
      }
    },
    "stateVariables": {
      "MODULE_ROLE": {
        "details": "The salt domain. One module kind per factory, so the domain keeps      this address apart from any other CREATE2 deployer in the protocol."
      },
      "isApprovedImplementation": {
        "details": "Written only by the constructor and `setImplementation`, so it holds      every implementation in `implementationHistory` and nothing else."
      },
      "isLegacyImplementation": {
        "details": "Written once, by the constructor. No setter, so the set cannot grow      after deployment and no later call can widen what `registerModule`      recognises."
      }
    },
    "version": 1
  },
